<h2 id="how-to-choose">How to choose in five questions</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Does it work with your EHR?</strong> Copy-and-paste is fine for a solo practice. Groups usually need notes written straight into the record.</li>
<li><strong>Is it built for your specialty?</strong> Check the templates for your note types — a psychiatry intake and an orthopaedic follow-up need very different structure.</li>
<li><strong>Where does patient data go?</strong> In the US, ask for a signed BAA (HIPAA). In the UK, check UK GDPR and NHS requirements. In Canada, provincial privacy law (such as PHIPA in Ontario). In Australia, the Privacy Act.</li>
<li><strong>How much editing does each note need?</strong> A scribe only saves time if you trust the draft. Test it on your own real-world consultations during the free trial.</li>
<li><strong>What does it really cost?</strong> Look for note caps, annual-only prices and per-encounter fees — not just the headline monthly price.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout"><b>Before you start any trial:</b> tell patients the consultation is being recorded and get their consent, as your local rules require.</div>

